# Pin committed TypeScript build outputs to LF line endings.
#
# The D-02 freshness check rebuilds the .ts sources to a temp directory and
# compares the result byte-for-byte against the committed .js. If a Windows
# checkout stored those committed .js files with CRLF while a macOS/Linux CI
# rebuild emits LF, the comparison would fail on line endings alone — a false
# "stale build" signal that masks (or is mistaken for) real drift. Pinning the
# committed build outputs to LF here, together with tsconfig "newLine": "lf",
# keeps the freshness diff deterministic and meaningful across platforms.
install/*.js    text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.js text eol=lf
hooks/*.js      text eol=lf

# Pin the committed generated catalog to LF for the same reason: the catalog
# generator emits literal "\n", and its byte-stability (the `npm run generate:catalog`
# no-op contract and the DOCS-01 reproducibility test) must hold across platforms.
docs/catalog/README.md text eol=lf

# Pin ALL remaining committed text sources to LF. Root cause (2026-07-21, CI run
# 29824410110): the two pins above only covered committed .js build outputs and the
# generated catalog. Every other text file — agent-factory role/workflow markdown,
# .ts sources, install templates, JSON, YAML — had no eol pin, so on the windows-latest
# runner (Git for Windows core.autocrlf=true) they checked out CRLF. Line-anchored
# regexes and byte-exact oracles over those files then false-fire: SC-5's
# guard_context_writes calibration cases (scripts/check-foundation-guards.test.ts,
# incl. the Phase 20 "prose naming context-io.ts + path stays GREEN (A3)" case and the
# real-tree smoke test) failed on windows-latest, along with context-io.test.ts's
# function-span byte-hash oracle, install.test.ts's marker-line matching, and every
# freshness/catalog/ASVS reproducibility check — all CRLF-vs-LF false positives, not
# real drift. Pinning every text extension to LF here makes checkout content identical
# on every OS, so these guards observe the same bytes everywhere.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.mts  text eol=lf
*.cts  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf

# Pin the tracked text files that carry NO extension, plus the SVG and JSONL classes, to LF.
# Root cause (2026-09-18, CI run 35394268365, windows-latest): the per-extension pins above left
# 13 tracked text files unpinned — `.gitattributes`, `.gitignore`, the `.gitkeep` placeholders,
# LICENSE, NOTICE, agent-factory/VERSION, five brand/*.svg and a fixture *.jsonl — so under the
# runner's core.autocrlf=true they checked out CRLF, and `check:nul-bytes` (which forbids 0x0d)
# refused every one of them: "152 byte(s) across 13 of the 2364 tracked file(s)". Not drift, not a
# NUL — the checkout. The set below is DERIVED, not guessed: `git ls-files --eol` rows whose index
# side is `i/lf` and whose `eol` attribute `git check-attr` reports unspecified; a case in
# scripts/check-nul-bytes.test.ts re-derives that set on every host and refuses a tracked text
# file that is not pinned, so the next unpinned class reds here and not only on windows-latest
# (plan 33-05, CAP-02).
*.svg          text eol=lf
*.jsonl        text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitkeep       text eol=lf
LICENSE        text eol=lf
NOTICE         text eol=lf
VERSION        text eol=lf
